        "title": "Case Studies and Cyber Safety Emergency Toolkit",
        "content": "---\n\n### 17.6.1 Four Detailed Real-Life Indian Student Case Studies\n\n#### Case Study 1: Kunal (Pune, 21) — Digital Arrest Scam Evaded\n\n```\n[Fake ED Video Call] ──> [Threats of Asset Freeze] ──> [Kunal Recalls Module 17 Rules] ──> [Demands Physical Station Warrant and Cuts Call] ──> [Dialed 1930] ──> [Saved Rs.50,000]\n```\n\n- **Background:** Kunal, a 3rd-year Computer Engineering student living in a flat in Kothrud, Pune, received an unexpected WhatsApp video call from a caller dressed in a Police Uniform. The background screen displayed official Enforcement Directorate (ED) logos and banners.\n- **The Threat Setup:** The scammer claimed Kunal's Aadhaar details were linked to a seized syndicate account in Canara Bank Hyderabad holding Rs.4.2 crores in illegal betting proceeds. He declared Kunal under \"digital Arrest\" under the Money Laundering Act and demanded immediate Rs.50,000 transfer into an \"ED Escrow Verification Wallet\".\n- **The Counter-Action:** Kunal recalled *Rupaiya 101 Module 17* core legal rule: **\"Digital Arrest does NOT exist under Indian Criminal Law. Authorities never conduct interrogations via video call.\"** Kunal calmly looked into the webcam and stated: *\"Inspector Sir, please send my arrest warrant from Deccan Gymkhana Police Station. I will come to the ACP office to sign a physical statement.\"*\n- **Outcome:** The scammer instantly cut the video call and blocked Kunal's phone number. Kunal immediately reported the scammer's number and video screenshot on **cybercrime.gov.in** and alerted helpline **1930**. Kunal successfully protected his Rs.50,000 semester internship stipend!\n\n---\n\n#### Case Study 2: Meera (Bengaluru, 22) — Security Deposit Recovery\n\n```\n[Landlord Withheld Rs.35,000 Deposit] ──> [Meera Produced Move-in Video Audit] ──> [Disputed Illegal Paint Charges] ──> [Dispatched Formal Legal Notice] ──> [Recovered Rs.35,000 IN FULL]\n```\n\n- **Background:** Meera vacated her rented flat in Koramangala, Bengaluru after 12 months. The landlord deducted Rs.35,000 for repainting, sanitization, and lock scuffs.\n- **Meera's Defense:** She produced her move-in video audit showing pre-existing paint fading and tile scuffs. She cited the Model Tenancy Act's wear-and-tear provision.\n- **Meera's Legal Notice:** She sent a formal legal notice via registered post demanding full refund within 15 days or Consumer Court action.\n- **Outcome:** The landlord returned the full Rs.35,000 within 10 days to avoid legal proceedings.",
        "color": "#10B981",
        "emoji": "🔒",
